WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.377
The most dangerous SQL logic errors are often the ones that do not produce an error message.

00:00:05.377 --> 00:00:12.632
PostgreSQL may run the query successfully, return rows, and make the output look completely reasonable.

00:00:12.632 --> 00:00:14.972
But the rule can still be wrong.

00:00:14.972 --> 00:00:20.097
One common trap is mixing AND and OR without parentheses.

00:00:20.097 --> 00:00:22.862
Another is using equals NULL.

00:00:22.862 --> 00:00:29.132
Another is forgetting that ordinary not-equal also becomes UNKNOWN when the field is NULL.

00:00:29.132 --> 00:00:34.052
NOT IN can produce surprising results when the list contains NULL.

00:00:34.052 --> 00:00:40.012
CASE branches can be ordered incorrectly so a broad condition hides a specific one.

00:00:40.012 --> 00:00:43.477
Blank text can be mistaken for valid data.

00:00:43.477 --> 00:00:46.402
A zero can be confused with missingness.

00:00:46.402 --> 00:00:50.927
This is why professional SQL work includes adversarial testing.

00:00:50.927 --> 00:00:57.627
Instead of testing only normal rows, intentionally test the records most likely to break the logic.

00:00:57.627 --> 00:01:00.822
Create or identify a row with NULL.

00:01:00.822 --> 00:01:02.307
One with zero. One with an empty string.

00:01:02.307 --> 00:01:06.122
One with only spaces. One exactly on the threshold.

00:01:06.122 --> 00:01:08.512
One that satisfies two CASE branches.

00:01:08.512 --> 00:01:13.577
One with explicit FALSE. This is similar to testing software.

00:01:13.577 --> 00:01:18.712
Happy-path data tells you the query works in the easiest situation.

00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:24.052
Edge cases tell you whether the rule is actually reliable.

00:01:24.052 --> 00:01:27.157
A query that “runs” is only the beginning.

00:01:27.157 --> 00:01:31.961
A query that has survived boundary testing is much closer to something you can trust.
